WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:05.420
GARY SCHMITT: The United States faces security threats from multiple states and groups,

00:00:05.420 --> 00:00:08.020
and in multiple regions of the world.

00:00:08.020 --> 00:00:12.500
But it does so at a time when public opinion
is less supportive of American efforts to

00:00:12.500 --> 00:00:16.100
address those threats than anytime in decades.

00:00:21.720 --> 00:00:25.480
Since the inconclusive wars in Iraq and Afghanistan

00:00:25.499 --> 00:00:30.669
and the 2008 financial crash, America’s
appetite for global security leadership has

00:00:30.669 --> 00:00:32.880
dropped dramatically.

00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:38.000
When he entered office, President Obama pledged
to rebuild the country by moving away from

00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:40.460
“nation-building abroad” to “nation-building
at-home.”

00:00:40.460 --> 00:00:44.080
BARACK OBAMA: ...and use the rest to do some nation-building right here at home!

00:00:44.080 --> 00:00:49.120
GARY SCHMITT: Likewise, when running for president, Donald Trump questioned the wars America had been

00:00:49.120 --> 00:00:52.460
fighting and the value of America’s alliances abroad.

00:00:52.460 --> 00:00:55.760
DONALD TRUMP: ...have dragged us into foreign wars that have made us less safe...

00:00:55.760 --> 00:01:00.660
GARY SCHMITT: Both presidents believed that a forward-leaning American military presence is costly

00:01:00.670 --> 00:01:07.409
and at times counterproductive, involving us in conflicts
and competitions with other powers that are

00:01:07.409 --> 00:01:11.390
not, they argued, in our national interest.

00:01:11.390 --> 00:01:13.210
But the policies of retrenchment have not

00:01:13.210 --> 00:01:15.781
brought us greater peace and stability.

00:01:15.781 --> 00:01:21.310
In the cases of Russia, China, and Iran, efforts
to normalize relations have not lessened tensions.

00:01:21.310 --> 00:01:27.450
To the contrary, each nation has read US policies
and cuts to defense spending as signs of American

00:01:27.450 --> 00:01:29.369
weakness and decline.

00:01:29.369 --> 00:01:35.580
If anything, American retrenchment has fueled
the ambitions of Russia, China and Iran...

00:01:35.580 --> 00:01:37.120
not appeased them.

00:01:37.130 --> 00:01:46.639
As a result, we face growing uncertainty in
three regions of the world—Europe, Asia

00:01:46.639 --> 00:01:52.719
and the Middle East—that American statecraft
has long understood to be the linchpins of

00:01:52.719 --> 00:01:54.949
global peace and security.

00:01:54.949 --> 00:01:57.619
However, that begs the question of why the

00:01:57.619 --> 00:01:59.719
United States should care now?

00:01:59.720 --> 00:02:04.380
After all, the threats posed by the three
states are far from American shores.

00:02:04.380 --> 00:02:09.239
Some even argue that the US might concede
greater sway in a region to these rising powers

00:02:09.240 --> 00:02:13.400
in the expectation that they will then become
satisfied powers.

00:02:13.400 --> 00:02:15.980
But history suggests otherwise.

00:02:15.990 --> 00:02:20.790
New levels of dominance in a region inevitably
introduces new interests and new concerns

00:02:20.790 --> 00:02:22.599
for the rising power.

00:02:22.599 --> 00:02:26.650
Moreover, states that neighbor the rising
power will have an interest in pushing back

00:02:26.650 --> 00:02:31.060
against such dominance or, if unable to do
so, appeasing the rising power.

00:02:31.060 --> 00:02:35.780
In either case, the rising power will have
created the kinds of tensions and instability

00:02:35.780 --> 00:02:40.460
that inevitably prevent a rising power from
becoming a satisfied power.

00:02:40.460 --> 00:02:45.360
The security dilemma posed by a rising power
is further fueled by the specific character

00:02:45.370 --> 00:02:46.470
of that state.

00:02:46.470 --> 00:02:51.269
In this case, all three countries—Russia,
China and Iran—are illiberal and present

00:02:51.269 --> 00:02:54.740
themselves as challengers to the democratic
West.

00:02:54.740 --> 00:03:00.000
By their very nature, they rest uneasy in
a world now dominated by liberal trade and

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:04.409
politics, and where the dominant power is
still the United States.

00:03:04.409 --> 00:03:07.230
It’s undeniable that American prosperity

00:03:07.230 --> 00:03:11.969
and security are enhanced when America is
the dominant power in Europe, Asia and the

00:03:11.969 --> 00:03:12.980
Middle East.

00:03:12.980 --> 00:03:18.160
However, with the fall of the Soviet Union
in 1991, America took its eye off the ball.

00:03:18.160 --> 00:03:22.790
As a result, some Americans started to believe
in the “end of history,” the idea that

00:03:22.790 --> 00:03:27.580
the ideological conflicts of the 19th and
20th century had come to a close.

00:03:27.580 --> 00:03:31.780
Others simply wanted the peace dividend that
came with the end of the Cold War.

00:03:31.780 --> 00:03:36.580
With no serious challengers to threaten American
security, support for military spending and

00:03:36.580 --> 00:03:38.819
global deployments eroded.

00:03:38.819 --> 00:03:42.620
Americans continued to profess the values
of a global leadership, but they thought the

00:03:42.620 --> 00:03:49.560
nation could do as much while shrinking the
military and reducing its overseas presence.

00:03:49.560 --> 00:03:51.560
FIREMAN: Battalion One to Manhattan.

00:03:51.560 --> 00:03:57.320
We just had a plane crash into the upper floors of the World Trade Center.

00:03:57.320 --> 00:04:03.879
Most Americans believe that 9/11 changed things,
stirring Americans from their delusions about

00:04:03.879 --> 00:04:07.290
the dawn of an era of absolute security.

00:04:07.290 --> 00:04:12.319
But despite substantial investments in fighting
the wars in Iraq and Afghanistan, successive

00:04:12.320 --> 00:04:17.460
US presidents declined to make the long-term
investments necessary to modernize the military.

00:04:17.460 --> 00:04:23.340
As a result, the American military is losing
its qualitative edge and is too small to address

00:04:23.340 --> 00:04:26.940
the various global challenges it now faces.

00:04:26.940 --> 00:04:32.120
Once the envy of the world, America’s military
can no longer confidently “shock and awe”

00:04:32.120 --> 00:04:34.080
its adversaries.

00:04:34.080 --> 00:04:36.351
To restore the world order, Congress and the

00:04:36.351 --> 00:04:38.840
White House must adopt a long-term perspective.

00:04:38.840 --> 00:04:42.760
The US cannot focus on what changes—threats
and technologies.

00:04:42.760 --> 00:04:47.440
Instead, America must recognize what remains
constant—American security interests and

00:04:47.440 --> 00:04:49.000
political principles.

00:04:49.000 --> 00:04:51.520
Defending the liberal order is in our interest.

00:04:51.530 --> 00:04:56.330
And it is best done with allies who, in exchange
for American security guarantees, have by

00:04:56.330 --> 00:05:02.419
and large allowed the United States to construct
a global order that is both in our image, and to

00:05:02.420 --> 00:05:04.020
our advantage.

00:05:07.180 --> 00:05:10.860
To learn more about national security and American global leadership,

00:05:10.860 --> 00:05:15.880
check out the link to my new book "Rise of the Revisionists" in the description below.

